<article class="env-lemma" id="00JW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00JW">Lemma <span data-tag="00JW">10.58.2</span>.</a> A graded ring $S$ is Noetherian if and only if $S_0$ is Noetherian and $S_{+}$ is finitely generated as an ideal of $S$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is clear that if $S$ is Noetherian then $S_0 = S/S_{+}$ is Noetherian and $S_{+}$ is finitely generated. Conversely, assume $S_0$ is Noetherian and $S_{+}$ finitely generated as an ideal of $S$. Pick generators $S_{+} = (f_1, \ldots , f_ n)$. By decomposing the $f_ i$ into homogeneous pieces we may assume each $f_ i$ is homogeneous. By Lemma <a href="/tag/07Z4" data-tag="07Z4">10.58.1</a> we see that $S_0[X_1, \ldots X_ n] \to S$ sending $X_ i$ to $f_ i$ is surjective. Thus $S$ is Noetherian by Lemma <a href="/tag/00FN" data-tag="00FN">10.31.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>